WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:04.980
We know there are some deleted scenes from
It: Chapter Two out there somewhere, as well

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.140
as numerous key moments from Stephen King's
original novel that didn't make the cut.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.420
With that in mind, let's examine some intriguing
scenes that we didn't get to see.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:18.560
It is a very long novel, even when you're
approaching it with the idea of turning the

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.320
story into two separate films.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:25.480
King's book has the luxury of taking its time
with the characters, sometimes even meandering

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:29.970
a bit to allow us to get to know them and
the world in which they exist, and that means

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:34.530
while some things were left out of the films
entirely, others were heavily abbreviated

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.340
for the sake of moving along the plot.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:42.180
This is particularly true of the way the adult
members of the Losers' Club are introduced.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.199
As in King's novel, we meet them in their
adult forms as they each receive a phone call

00:00:46.199 --> 00:00:50.780
from Mike Hanlon, but we don't see much of
what they do in the immediate aftermath, because

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.180
the film just needs to get them to Derry quickly.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:55.700
"You need to come home."

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:01.200
In the book, King spends a whole chapter with
each member of the Club, shedding a little

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.780
more light on how they each cope with the
trauma of the phone call.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:09.920
This is particularly memorable in the case
of Ben Hanscom, who chooses to handle it by

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.831
heading to a local bar, asking the bartender
to fill a beer stein with whiskey, and then

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:19.840
squeezing lemon juice into his nostrils to
shock his body into taking more alcohol.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.500
It's an unforgettable scene, but the film
just didn't have room for it.

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:29.240
In the film, Bill Denbrough's wife Audra Phillips
only appears in the scene where Bill gets

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:34.500
the phone call summoning him back to Derry,
but in the novel, she has an expanded role.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.320
As in the film, Audra is an actress who sometimes
works on film adaptations of Bill's stories,

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.060
but in the book, she's also drawn to Derry
as she becomes increasingly fearful over what

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:46.480
might happen to her husband.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:52.080
This leads to a confrontation with Beverly's
husband, Tom Rogan, who abducts her and takes

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.000
her down into It's lair.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:02:00.540
When It reveals its true form, the "deadlights,"
Tom dies on the spot, and Audra falls into

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:01.740
a catatonic state.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:06.820
She survives the final battle, but she remains
comatose until Bill can revive her by putting

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:11.610
her on the back of his childhood bike, Silver,
and riding free through the streets to recapture

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:13.020
some of the joy of his youth.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.840
“Hi ho silver away!"

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.600
Bill and Audra kiss and then move on with
their lives.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:27.260
It's a satisfying subplot, but it's one that
the film didn't seem to have space for.

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:34.840
In It Chapter Two, both Bill Denbrough and
Beverly Marsh are revealed to be married,

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:37.700
but their respective spouses only get one
scene.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:43.720
In the book, the spouses are used as more
direct plot devices, particularly Tom Rogan,

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.360
Beverly's husband who repeats the cycle of
abuse she endured with her father years earlier.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.960
In the film, we see Tom get violent with Beverly
when she announces she has to go to Derry.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.660
Then she fights him off, and he's out of the
picture for the rest of the film.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:03.900
In King's novel, though, Tom's abusive obsession
carries him much further, as he plans to travel

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:09.350
to Derry himself so he can punish and ultimately
kill Beverly, as well as Bill, in a jealous

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:09.840
rage.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:12.500
"You're nothing without me, you know that,
right?!"

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:18.680
This leads to It latching on to Tom's violent
persona, much as It did with Henry Bowers,

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:23.741
and using him to kidnap Bill's wife and bring
her into It's lair as a lure for the Losers'

00:03:23.741 --> 00:03:24.240
Club.

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.360
That's the end of the line for Tom, as he
dies after seeing It's "deadlights."

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:35.340
Tom's violent subplot was probably just one
side-villain too many for the film, so his

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.160
expanded role wasn't included.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:43.220
One of the keys to It as both a novel and a film adaptation is the sense that we can

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.580
always learn more about "It," but we can't
learn everything.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:52.230
The creature is just too ancient, too strange,
and too terrifying for us to ever fully penetrate

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:52.880
its origins.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:58.330
King's novel digs a little deeper into the
entity's backstory than the films do, but

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:03.150
Chapter Two still shows us certain things,
like the meteor carrying It crashing into

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:03.640
Earth.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.320
We even get a tease of Pennywise's early circus
days.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.480
"You haven't saved any of them."

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:17.080
According to director Andy Muschietti , though,
we almost got a little more in the form of

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:21.340
a flashback that would've taken the film about
400 years into the past.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:23.640
He told Consequence of Sound:

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:26.780
"There's a scene that we shot that's in the
1600s.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:31.720
I decided not to put it in the film because
it was a little confusing.

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.620
You know, the problem is that people sometimes
want to know a little more, but if you give

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:39.000
them too much, then they're disappointed.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.340
It's like a magic trick in a way."

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.260
In both the book and the film, Mike Hanlon
is the driving force that brings the adult

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.070
Losers' Club back to Derry.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:52.860
He's the one who stays behind, the one who
does the research, and in the film, the one

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.800
who discovers all the finer details of the
Ritual of Chud and how to carry it out.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:03.380
Because of this, while the other Losers get
to spend a lot of solo time out in Derry,

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:08.250
we see more of Mike when he's teamed up with
other characters, and therefore, his flashbacks

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.380
to the past aren't quite as substantive.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:12.680
That wasn't always the case, though.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:18.000
According to cinematographer Checco Varese,
a deleted scene from Chapter Two features

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.180
Mike returning to the site of the house fire
that killed his parents.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:22.100
He said:

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:25.380
"Yeah, we shot [a flashback], and it's super
beautiful.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:29.500
There is a little hint in the movie right
now when there's a little baby Mike looking

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.090
at some wall on fire.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:32.760
But that's a very brief moment."

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:39.520
Varese added that little bit would've turned,
quote, "into a complex scene," but sadly,

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:40.560
it just didn't make the cut.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:45.800
In It Chapter Two, the members of the Losers'
Club head out to explore Derry as adults in

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:50.080
the hopes of finding a token of their memories
to use in the Ritual of Chud.

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:51.360
"And these pieces?

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:53.020
These artifacts.

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:54.900
That's why we're here.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:58.740
They are what you'll sacrifice."

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:04.090
In the book, the Ritual is a bit less concrete
than that, and they're exploring the town

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:08.300
again simply to reconnect with their own memories
and understand why they came back.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:13.680
This rediscovery takes similar form in both
the book and the film, but certain scenes

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:16.360
are either heavily altered or left out entirely.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.820
One of the most memorable moments from the
novel that didn't make it to the film features

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:26.670
Ben Hanscom exploring not Derry High School,
but the library where he spent so much time

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:27.340
as a child.

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:33.680
While there, he meets Pennywise costumed as
Dracula, with a mouthful of razor blades that

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.820
cut chunks of It's own flesh as the creature
talks.

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:41.840
That's a horrifying image, and it also leads
Ben to learn what exactly happened to their

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:46.660
absent friend, Stanley Uris, who cut his wrists
after getting the phone call from Mike.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:53.900
In both the book and the film, Pennywise's
scheming against the Losers' Club includes

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:58.790
drafting the bully Henry Bowers back into
It's service and helping Henry to escape the

00:06:58.790 --> 00:07:01.840
asylum where he's been confined for nearly
30 years.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:06.800
Henry returns to Derry and launches his own
violent assault on the Losers' Club, and in

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:12.590
the film, he manages to stab Eddie Kaspbrak
through the cheek and then attack Mike Hanlon

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:13.420
in the library.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:18.480
In the novel, Henry's attack is a little more
successful, as he's able to injure Mike enough

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:22.060
to put him in the hospital and keep him out
of the Ritual of Chud.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:27.640
This gets even more complicated in the novel's
climax, as Mike is attacked in his hospital

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:32.980
bed by a nurse possessed by It, and the other
Losers must use their combined wills to send

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:35.020
him strength to fend off his attacker.

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:39.260
While it's nice to have the Club all together
for the Ritual in the film, it's a shame that

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:42.260
we don't get to see Mike's solo struggle during
the climax.

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:48.720
In the novel, a version of the Ritual of Chud
happens twice, and both times, it's a metaphysical

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.540
battle led by Bill Denbrough.

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:54.940
When Bill does the Ritual as a child, he has
a vision of a turtle who gives him a little

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:56.720
advice during his spiritual journey.

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:03.020
The turtle is named Maturin, and he's an important
part of the King cosmology that was, unfortunately,

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:06.280
left out of It Chapter One save for an Easter
egg or two.

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:11.880
As it turns out, though, there was almost
a scene in Chapter Two that featured the legendary

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:12.380
turtle.

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:14.840
Andy Muschietti explained to Consequence of
Sound:

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:20.020
"When you see [James] McAvoy confronting his
fear in the flooded basement, and he kills

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:25.220
the notion of guilt by killing himself as
a kid, he jumps back in the water.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:31.080
He's lost, there is no way out, and suddenly,
the eyes of Pennywise [...] come out of the

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:31.580
dark.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:36.000
But it's not Pennywise, it's the turtle that
is swimming by him.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.600
[...] Very soon after, the kids are swimming
after it.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:43.240
So, McAvoy follows them toward the light,
and he emerges back in the cavern."

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:47.980
In other words, it would've been a pretty
weird scene, but hey, this is a movie about

00:08:47.980 --> 00:08:51.060
a demon clown, so strange stuff comes with
the territory.

00:08:51.780 --> 00:08:56.880
The final confrontation between the Losers'
Club and It in Chapter Two is a complicated,

00:08:56.890 --> 00:09:00.860
multi-layered affair, and it features the
creature morphing into a twisted version of

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:03.620
the spider-form that Stephen King describes
in the novel.

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:08.300
But there's one aspect of that battle that
was left out of the film entirely, either

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:11.410
for the sake of brevity, simplicity, or both.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:17.040
In the novel, the battle with It is prolonged
and difficult, and as the Losers managed to

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:23.490
drive It back deeper into its lair, they make
a horrifying discovery a trail of black eggs

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:25.760
the size of ostrich eggs.

00:09:25.960 --> 00:09:29.640
Yeah, It is giving birth to demonic offspring.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:34.260
So the Losers immediately decide the eggs
must be destroyed, and that task falls to

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:39.430
Ben Hanscom, who crushes the eggs and then
chases after the rat-sized spider creatures

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:41.140
that crawl out of each one.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:46.040
It's a horrifying scene in the novel that
adds an extra layer of wild violence to the

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:50.910
final battle, but it does seem to function
as more of a side quest than anything else.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:56.740
In It Chapter Two, the Losers are able to
defeat It by making it assume the shape of

00:09:56.740 --> 00:09:59.860
something small and weak, then crushing its
heart.

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:03.300
"Look at you.

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:09.940
You're all grown up."

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:15.760
With this process complete, they must then
race out of It's lair as it collapses around

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:20.360
them, and after escaping, they discover the
house on Neibolt Street has been destroyed

00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:21.140
along with It.

00:10:21.710 --> 00:10:26.560
Stephen King's novel takes a similar approach
in that it also alters the physical landscape

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:32.740
of Derry in tandem with It's destruction,
but in the book, things are way more apocalyptic.

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:38.080
Because It's presence and malevolence is woven
so deeply into the fabric of the town, It's

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:43.180
death is matched by an enormous storm that
blows through Derry while the Losers' Club

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:45.100
engage the entity in battle.

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:50.860
By the time the fight is over and It is defeated,
the Losers discover that much of Derry's downtown

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:56.060
has collapsed, and Mike theorizes that this
means the town will die along with It.

00:10:56.600 --> 00:11:01.240
It's the same concept as the film, but here,
things are on a much bigger scale.

